
  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153muserId[39m is data the client already has (so it can be a parameter) or data the server produces (in
  which case a parameter is the wrong shape and it belongs in the return value instead).

  - [1mIt's the username[0m — cheapest, zero new plumbing, but then [38;5;153mlogin(username, password, userId)[39m is passing the same
    value twice and "track who logged in" is already satisfied by the existing log line. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mServer returns it[0m — [38;5;153muserId[39m becomes a field on the return value ([38;5;153m{ success, user, userId }[39m), not a parameter. Correct
    if the ID is a database key, but it means the request as literally worded ("add a parameter") isn't what gets
    built. Cheap now, awkward to undo once callers read the field.
  - [1mClient-generated correlation ID[0m — the handler generates an ID before calling [38;5;153mlogin[39m and passes it in, so the same
    login attempt can be traced across client and server. This is the only reading where a [3mparameter[0m is genuinely the
    right shape. Costs a new ID-generation decision and, if it should survive a page reload, storage — which would push
    this past bounded.

  [1mMy recommendation: the correlation-ID reading[0m, but only if tracing attempts is what you're after. It's the one where a
  parameter is actually correct rather than redundant or misplaced. If what you want is "know which account logged in,"
  the server-returns-it option is the honest shape and I'd change the return value instead of the signature.

  One thing worth naming either way: [38;5;153mlogin[39m currently logs the identifier to the console, so whatever [38;5;153muserId[39m holds will
  end up in browser logs. Fine for an opaque ID, less so if it's an email or anything account-identifying.
